WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.162
In analytical modeling, the grain question remains essential.

00:00:04.162 --> 00:00:07.607
A fact table represents measurable events.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:12.537
Before adding columns, state exactly what one fact row means.

00:00:12.537 --> 00:00:17.872
A star schema connects the fact table directly to descriptive dimensions.

00:00:17.872 --> 00:00:21.977
This often makes analytical queries easier to understand.

00:00:21.977 --> 00:00:27.562
A snowflake design normalizes some dimensions into related sub-dimensions.

00:00:27.562 --> 00:00:31.932
That can reduce redundancy but increases the number of joins.

00:00:31.932 --> 00:00:35.237
Neither pattern removes the need to define grain.

00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:40.552
For our clinic example, one possible fact grain is one appointment-service line.

00:00:40.552 --> 00:00:45.957
That sentence tells us what each fact row represents and which measures, keys,

00:00:45.957 --> 00:00:48.082
and dimensions can belong there.

00:00:48.082 --> 00:00:52.652
Always define fact grain before designing an analytical table.

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:57.007
Operational and analytical models optimize for different work.

00:00:57.007 --> 00:01:05.477
An OLTP schema protects transactional consistency, while a star schema often makes reporting simpler and

00:01:05.477 --> 00:01:09.062
faster. A mature data platform may intentionally use both:

00:01:09.062 --> 00:01:13.233
normalized operational sources feeding dimensional analytical models.
